 # Hatchet TypeScript SDK
 
+## Isolated candidate: owned worker lifecycle
+
+This local candidate adds an opt-in contract to `HatchetClient` from
+`@hatchet-dev/typescript-sdk/v1/index.js`. It is not an upstream SDK guarantee.
+Existing callers that omit `workerLifecycle` keep their existing behavior.
+
+Pass `HatchetV1ClientOptions.workerLifecycle` with a startup AbortSignal and
+absolute `deadlineAt` fixed before construction, a distinct `lifetimeSignal`,
+and `cleanupTimeoutMs: 15000`. The intended startup deadline is
+`Date.now() + 15000`. `getStartupInfo()` caches the constructor tenant probe and
+one admission version read, returning `{ tenantVersion, engineVersion }`.
+Version validation remains the consumer's responsibility; worker startup keeps
+its two engine-version gates.
+
+Owned acquisition is single-use: one modern non-durable worker, ordinary tasks,
+no batch/durable declarations or legacy branch. Failed acquisition does not
+reopen that slot. The owned client does not support legacy, run-result or durable
+listeners, or promise ownership of arbitrary other SDK operations. Both new
+methods reject without extra I/O when lifecycle ownership was not enabled.
+
+Owned tenant/admission-probe and pause REST calls use the ordinary built-in
+Axios HTTP/1 adapter with per-request `maxRedirects: 0` and the phase signal.
+Both configured and effective base URLs must be canonical HTTP(S) URLs.
+Custom adapters/transports, HTTP/2, streaming responses, caller request
+transforms and CancelToken are rejected before the constructor probe. Effective
+defaults are rechecked before each owned dispatch; stock request serialization,
+headers, authentication, JSON parsing, TLS, validators and agents are preserved.
+Redirects are rejected even when a validator accepts 3xx, and no next hop is
+followed. The HTTP join retains the original operation and awaits only a public
+native `ClientRequest.closed` or `close` event identified consistently through
+`response.request`, `reason.request` or `reason.response.request`. A missing,
+non-native or inconsistent handle fails closed;
+wrapper destruction, socket state and Axios rejection are not close receipts.
+The original rejection cause is preserved, including an undefined cause.
+Externally replaced/mutated API instances, interceptors or custom transports
+are outside this exact supported chain. Shared agents are not owned or closed.
+Ordinary late signal cancellation can still discard the outward request handle;
+the observer used by tests never repairs that runtime gap. The scoped Oracle
+approval permits only isolated qualification of this restriction, not complete
+SDK ownership, active adoption or normal pool release on an unsafe outcome.
+
+`worker.stop()` and `client.dispose()` share one fresh cleanup deadline starting
+at the first terminal transition. Startup cancellation does not cancel the
+cleanup signal. Successful startup detaches the old startup deadline; lifetime
+cancellation remains active. `handleKill:false` installs no process handlers.
+Startup commit denotes registration and consumer installation, not engine or
+application readiness.
+Owned signal handlers request joined shutdown and remove their exact references.
+
+Shutdown retains original handler executions independently of cancellable
+futures, and drains STARTED plus one outcome obligation for admitted tasks.
+Terminal transition forbids new dispatch/retry series; a failed COMPLETED
+transmission never becomes a replacement FAILED event. An uncooperative handler
+still prevents disposal from completing. Heartbeat generations terminate and
+join before reconnect can start another generation.
+
+A fulfilled disposal is the local ownership receipt for this supported scope.
+A rejection is not permission to release dependent SQL pools. In particular,
+unknown native settlement retains channels and fails closed; neither an absent
+trailer, an AbortError, nor elapsed time is interpreted as proof of no native
+operation. Local closure does not prove remote registration rollback or that
+the engine did not receive a task event. This candidate still requires isolated
+qualification, clean reconstruction and independent review before adoption.
+
